s to arbitrary odd moduli. To describe Gordon’s theorem, some basic partition-theoretic notions are needed. Let $\\lambda $ $\\lambda $ be an integer partition, that is, $\\lambda =({\\lambda }_{1},{\\lambda }_{2},\\dots )$ $\\lambda =(\\lambda _1,\\lambda _2,\\dots )$ is a weakly decreasing sequence of nonnegative integers such that only finitely many ${\\lambda }_{i}$ $\\lambda _i$ are strictly positive [3]. Such positive ${\\lambda }_{i}$ $\\lambda _i$ are known as the parts of $\\lambda $ $\\lambda $, and it is standard convention to suppress the trailing sequence of zeros in a partition, so that $(5,3,3,2,2,2,1,0,\\dots )$ $(5,3,3,2,2,2,1,0,\\dots )$ is written as (5, 3, 3, 2, 2, 2, 1). If $|\\lambda |:={\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2}+\\cdots =N$ $\\vert \\lambda \\vert :=\\lambda _1+\\lambda _2+\\cdots =N$ then $\\lambda $ $\\lambda $ is said to be a partition of N, written as $\\lambda ⊢N$ $\\lambda \\vdash N$. $$\\begin{aligned} D_a^{(n)}(z,q)={\\left\\{ \\begin{array}{ll} F^{(n)}_{2a,0}(z,q) &  \\text { for } 0\\leqslant a\\leqslant \\lfloor n/2\\rfloor , \\\\ F^{(n)}_{2n-2a,0}(z,q) &  \\text { for } \\lceil n/2\\rceil \\leqslant a\\leqslant n. \\end{array}\\right. } \\end{aligned}$$\n\n(4.4)\n\nSpecialising $z=1$ $z=1$ and again appealing to Bressoud’s Rogers–Ramanujan-type identities for even moduli [9, 10] implies (4.3).\n\nBy Proposition A.2 with ${\\lambda }_{i}={k}_{i}+\\cdots +{k}_{n}$ $\\lambda _i=k_i+\\dots +k_n$ for $0⩽i⩽n$ $0\\leqslant i\\leqslant n$, Primc’s conjecture is expressible in terms of the affine Lie algebra ${\\mathrm{D}}_{n+1}^{(2)}$ $\\textrm{D}^{(2)}_{n+1}$ in the following manner, resolving one of the open problems from [38].\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - JorikJooken/occupancyFraction: Code related to the occupancy fraction of a graph · GitHub (https://github.com/JorikJooken/occupancyFraction)\nciteturn1search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; Jooken, Countere