ated to the Capparelli partition theorems\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Seifert form of chain type invertible singularities (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/339498294_Seifert_form_of_chain_type_invertible_singularities)\nciteturn1search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.8 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; In this paper, we resolve the Orlik-Randell conjecture. ... Theorem 1.2 (Modiﬁed Orlik-Randell conjecture).\nLet us be more precise\n\nabout what we actually prove. In Theorem 2.11 of [23], Orlik-Randell give an\n\nexplicit generators and relations presentation of the reduced integral cohomology\n\n𝐻*of the Milnor ﬁber of 𝑝 𝑎 as an abelian group. The proof is an inductive argument\n\nusing the cohomology long exact sequence of a pair (also see Remark 1.9 below). In\n\nparticular, the generators correspond to a basis of the relative cohomology group\n\nconstructed in their Theorem 2.10 via [21], which has special properties in regards\n\nto an ad-hoc monodromy action deﬁned in Lemma 2.6 (ii) (nothing else about this\n\nbasis is speciﬁed in [23]).\n\nThe generators of 𝐻*are linearly ordered in their notation but it appears to\n\nus that there is only a natural cyclic order. Taking the right number of adjacent\n\ngenerators provide a Z-basis of 𝐻*with special properties with respect to the\n\nmonodromy operator. The only possible way to interpret their conjecture is that\n\nthe dual basis to any of these bases in reduced integral homology 𝐻*=𝐻𝑜𝑚(𝐻*,Z)\n\nof the Milnor ﬁber is distinguished with the Seifert matrix in Equation 1.4.\n\nRemark 1.1. Notice that 𝐻*is consequently presented as the solution set of some\n\nlinear equations in a free abelian group (the relative homology group) with an induced\n\nbasis. Yet, the induced dual bases of 𝐻*as in the previous paragraph are not\n\nobtained by taking the corresponding adjacent basis elements of the ambient abelian\n\ngroup - they must be corrected (in a 